{"message_id":"15b1317a-89c6-4755-b114-c5340f84f81c","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:16:11.799533Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"runbook-2-3-001","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"Here's what I've got:\n\n**Core Tools:**\n- **Filesystem** — `ls`, `read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `glob`, `grep`\n- **Task management** — `write_todos`\n- **Calculation** — `calculate` (any arithmetic)\n- **Web search** — `search_web`\n\n**Specialist Agents (via `dispatch_by_capability`):**\n- **Architect** — C4 diagrams, ADRs, architecture reviews, feasibility checks\n- **Ideation** — generate alternatives, steelman opposing views\n- **Product Owner** — spec reviews, acceptance criteria refinement\n\n**Build Pipeline:**\n- **queue_build** — kick off a Forge build for an already-planned feature\n\n**Subagents:**\n- **`task`** — spawn isolated subagents for research, complex multi-step work, etc.\n- **`jarvis-reasoner`** — local reasoning pass (critic / researcher / planner modes) on a local model\n\n**Escalation:**\n- **`escalate_to_frontier`** — cloud frontier models (Gemini 3.1 Pro / Opus 4.7), only when you explicitly ask\n\n**Other:**\n- **`list_available_capabilities`** — check current fleet registry\n- **`get_calendar_events`** — stub for now (today / tomorrow / this week)\n\nThat's the full set for this session. What do you need help with?","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"runbook-2-3-001"},"correlation_id":"runbook-2-3-001","success":true}}
{"message_id":"cf43ffed-8de9-4f17-b6bf-634e58bea82a","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:27:04.183696Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"b11d25ec-0e0d-4b69-8208-9aa35744b1fa","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"The architect agent isn't online in the fleet right now. Here's the situation:\n\n**Available agents in the fleet:**\n- **Product Owner Agent** — registered and available (tools: `po_idea`, `po_extract`, `po_greenfield`, `po_evolve`, `po_impact`, `po_scope`, etc.)\n- **Architect Agent** — *not currently registered* in the fleet\n- **Ideation Agent** — *not currently registered*\n- **Forge** — *not currently registered*\n\nThe architect capability listed in my session prompt is offline. You can bring it back with:\n\n```\ncapabilities_refresh\n```\n\n—or have the architect come online and then tell me \"check again.\"\n\n**In the meantime**, I can still give you my take on the ADR-ARCH-001 question. Here it is:\n\n---\n\n**Yes, a budget-capped cloud escalation path is compatible with local-first.** Here's why:\n\n1. **Local-first means default, not absolute.** A budget cap + explicit consent preserves the invariant — the system *prefers* local but has a controlled escape hatch. The invariant is about direction, not rigidity.\n\n2. **Safeguards that make this work:**\n   - **Explicit user opt-in** per escalation (no silent cloud calls)\n   - **Per-request budget cap** (e.g., £0.50/request) so costs are predictable\n   - **Audit log** of every escalation (what was sent, cost, model used)\n   - **Monthly hard cap** (£20-50) with auto-shutoff when reached\n   - **Data scoping** — only the minimal context needed, not the full conversation\n\n3. **The real risk isn't the escape hatch — it's scope creep.** Without tight controls, \"occasional cloud use\" becomes the default and local-first becomes local-when-convenient. The budget cap alone isn't enough; you need the consent gate and audit trail.\n\n4. **Operational benefit:** For frontier-tier questions (complex reasoning, code generation on unfamiliar codebases), local 120B models genuinely can't compete. Forcing local-only on those tasks degrades output quality, which undermines the whole system's utility.\n\n**Bottom line:** The invariant should say \"local unless the user explicitly opts into cloud, with strict per-request and monthly budget caps and full auditability.\" That's local-first with teeth, not local-first with an open door.\n\nWant me to bring the architect online to get a formal ADR review on this?","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"b11d25ec-0e0d-4b69-8208-9aa35744b1fa"},"correlation_id":"b11d25ec-0e0d-4b69-8208-9aa35744b1fa","success":true}}
{"message_id":"01e42bda-6128-4ec9-87db-bdeef6c8973e","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:27:07.967443Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"ace95092-a393-46d7-83ef-fd259b799969","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"```json\n{\n  \"follow_ups\": [\n    \"Can you try bringing the architect agent online so we can get a formal review?\",\n    \"What would the actual ADR look like if we draft one for the budget-capped cloud escalation path?\",\n    \"How do other similar systems handle the local-first vs cloud escalation trade-off?\",\n    \"What specific safeguards would you recommend for the audit log — what data should we capture on each escalation?\",\n    \"If the architect is down, can we use the ideation agent to brainstorm alternative approaches to this problem instead?\"\n  ]\n}\n```","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"ace95092-a393-46d7-83ef-fd259b799969"},"correlation_id":"ace95092-a393-46d7-83ef-fd259b799969","success":true}}
{"message_id":"ccabd650-73f9-4876-b59b-a24eb578448d","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:36:40.489480Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"9782f9bd-9606-4e53-a838-5ed1274f8fea","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"Architect is back online and has weighed in. Here's the summary:\n\n**Verdict: Conditionally feasible** (confidence: 85%)\n\n**Key findings:**\n- The escalation path is technically achievable if implemented as an **opt-in mechanism**, not a default — preserving the local-first principle.\n- The real challenge isn't connectivity; it's the safeguards.\n\n**Risks flagged:**\n1. **Data leakage** — sensitive data leaving premises without explicit consent\n2. **Budget overrun** — per-request caps not enforced at the gateway\n3. **Operational complexity** — managing dual-mode routing (local vs. cloud)\n\n**Recommendations:**\n- **Explicit consent gate** — user must confirm before any data leaves premises\n- **BudgetController component** — enforce the £20-50 monthly cap at the infrastructure layer\n- **Escalate only when local fails** — only route to cloud when local models can't meet quality thresholds\n- **Audit logging** — track every escalation event (cost, data movement, model used)\n\n**Estimated complexity:** Medium — requires new budget enforcement and consent components.\n\nSo the architect agrees with my assessment in principle: the invariant should allow escalation, but only with tight controls. The key addition from the architect is the **BudgetController** as a dedicated infrastructure component — that's a concrete design element worth capturing if we draft an ADR update.\n\nWant to proceed with drafting an ADR amendment, or is there something else you'd like to explore?","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"9782f9bd-9606-4e53-a838-5ed1274f8fea"},"correlation_id":"9782f9bd-9606-4e53-a838-5ed1274f8fea","success":true}}
{"message_id":"eb72cae4-553b-4d14-8489-a4e3366c9938","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:36:44.984772Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"bfcaae3d-2308-4c8c-b995-ca72ca05df93","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"```json\n{\n  \"follow_ups\": [\n    \"Let's draft an ADR amendment for the budget-capped cloud escalation path — can you write it up?\",\n    \"What would the BudgetController component look like at a high level? Can you sketch its interface?\",\n    \"Should we also draft a separate ADR for the explicit consent gate, or roll it into the same document?\",\n    \"What happens when the monthly budget cap is reached — should the system gracefully degrade, or just block all cloud calls?\",\n    \"Can we get the architect to produce a C4 diagram showing the local-first routing with the cloud escalation path?\"\n  ]\n}\n```","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"bfcaae3d-2308-4c8c-b995-ca72ca05df93"},"correlation_id":"bfcaae3d-2308-4c8c-b995-ca72ca05df93","success":true}}
{"message_id":"21a5227d-fbf9-487f-be26-d04d76f76658","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:37:46.286615Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"4d08ddeb-95be-4271-b709-cba7a3840c12","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"That's a good question, but none of the registered agents are designed for runtime quality-gate failures during autobuild. Let me walk through what each would actually do:\n\n**Architect** — Could assess whether the failure indicates a drift from documented architecture, but that's a *post-mortem* analysis, not a runtime intervention. It doesn't have the authority or mechanism to stop or redirect a build.\n\n**Product Owner** — Could evaluate whether the failure changes the priority or scope of the feature, but again, that's a planning-level concern. It doesn't handle runtime build failures.\n\n**Forge** — This is the build pipeline itself. The quality gate failure would surface *to* Forge as an event, not *through* another agent. Forge is where the build runs; it's the source of the failure signal, not the resolver.\n\n**Ideation** — Could brainstorm workarounds, but has no integration with the build system to apply them.\n\n**The honest answer:** There's no handoff because there's no agent designed for this. A runtime quality-gate failure is an **operational event**, not an architectural or product question. The current agent fleet treats builds as fire-and-forget (queue and wait for notifications).\n\n**What would actually need to happen:**\n\n1. The quality gate failure emits an event (e.g., `pipeline.build-stage-failed.{feature_id}`)\n2. Jarvis receives it and assesses severity\n3. If it's a transient failure (flaky test, infra issue) → retry\n4. If it's a real defect → **stop the build**, notify the user, and *then* potentially route to the right agent:\n   - **Architect** if the failure suggests an architectural contradiction\n   - **Product Owner** if the failure changes acceptance criteria or scope\n   - **Ideation** if we need alternative approaches to unblock\n\nBut that's a **new capability** — a build incident responder — that doesn't exist in the current fleet. The closest thing is Jarvis itself handling it, since I'm the general-purpose agent and the event lands in my context.\n\nWant me to flag this as a gap? It feels like there should be a `forge_monitor` or `build_responder` capability that sits between the pipeline events and the specialist agents, deciding which path to take when things go wrong.","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"4d08ddeb-95be-4271-b709-cba7a3840c12"},"correlation_id":"4d08ddeb-95be-4271-b709-cba7a3840c12","success":true}}
{"message_id":"bdbbb90a-6a3d-46e8-ace8-3a04bed1e824","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:37:49.973675Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"fc9eb3e4-7622-4e14-bb61-2e33fd6225b2","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"```json\n{\n  \"follow_ups\": [\n    \"Yes, flag it as a gap — what would the build_responder capability need to look like?\",\n    \"Can you draft a capability spec for a forge_monitor or build_responder agent?\",\n    \"Should this be a new specialist agent, or should it be added as a mode to the existing architect agent?\",\n    \"What events should the build_responder listen to, and what actions should it be able to take?\",\n    \"If we built this, would it also handle build successes and progress updates, or just failures?\"\n  ]\n}\n```","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"fc9eb3e4-7622-4e14-bb61-2e33fd6225b2"},"correlation_id":"fc9eb3e4-7622-4e14-bb61-2e33fd6225b2","success":true}}
{"message_id":"1266600e-603c-4c3f-9a98-eaa3bd4d4f15","timestamp":"2026-05-12T14:38:44.635349Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"557da237-d1ed-4a66-a512-122eca7ab957","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"We started by checking available agents, then evaluated whether ADR-ARCH-001's local-first invariant should allow a budget-capped cloud escalation path — the architect agreed it's conditionally feasible with proper safeguards like explicit consent, a BudgetController component, and audit logging. We then identified a gap in the fleet: no agent is designed to handle runtime quality-gate failures during autobuilds, and discussed what a build_responder capability might look like.","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"557da237-d1ed-4a66-a512-122eca7ab957"},"correlation_id":"557da237-d1ed-4a66-a512-122eca7ab957","success":true}}
